    {"organization":"APS","title":"Otto Robert Frisch","url":"https://www.aps.org/programs/outreach/history/historicsites/frisch.cfm"}

definition:
    Austrian-British physicist (1904–1979), Lise Meitner's nephew and her partner in the Christmas 1938 forest walk where they worked out that Hahn's barium meant the nucleus had split — Frisch borrowed 'fission' from biology for the name. Weeks later he confirmed it experimentally with recoil bursts from neutron-blasted uranium. In 1940 his memorandum with Rudolf Peierls estimated a critical mass of pure U-235 at roughly kilograms, not tons — the calculation that first made atomic bombs seem buildable. He joined the British Mission at Los Alamos afterward.
